%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9F5EBBCC] ## What is a numerical method for integration?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_71DAC8CF] ### Euler's Method
%[text] The simplest method of approximating an integral is to start at a known value $(x\_0,y\_0)${"altText":"(x\_0,y\_0)"} and attempt to follow a function $y(x)${"altText":"y(x)"} that is changing at a known rate (slope) $\\frac{dy}{dx}=f(x)${"altText":"(dy)\/(dx)=f(x)"}. Let's move with constant step size $\\Delta x = h${"altText":"Delta x = h"}. This information allows us to create a piecewise-linear function:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y\_{\\text{approx}} = \\begin{cases} y\_0 + f(x\_0)(x-x\_0) & x\_0 \\leq x \< x\_0+h = x\_1 \\\\ y\_1 + f(x\_1)(x-x\_1) & x\_1 \\leq x \< x\_1+h = x\_2\\\\ \\vdots \\\\ y\_n+f(x\_n)(x-x\_n) & x\_n \\leq x \< x\_n+h = x\_{n+1} \\end{cases}${"altText":"y\_{approx} = cases: y\_0 + f(x\_0)(x-x\_0) for x\_0 \<= x \< x\_0+h = x\_1 ; y\_1 + f(x\_1)(x-x\_1) for x\_1 \<= x \< x\_1+h = x\_2; ... ; y\_n+f(x\_n)(x-x\_n) for x\_n \<= x \< x\_n+h = x\_{n+1}"},
%[text] where $y\_n = y\_{\\text{approx}}(x\_n)${"altText":"y\_n = y\_{approx}(x\_n)"}.
%[text] It is more common to organize this into a table:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\matrix{k & x\_k = x\_{k-1}+h  & \\;\\;\\;f(x\_k) & \\;\\;y\_{k+1} = y\_k+f(x\_k)h}${"altText":"matrix \[k, x\_k = x\_{k-1}+h, f(x\_k), y\_{k+1} = y\_k+f(x\_k)h\]"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\pmatrix{ 0 & x\_0 & f(x\_0) & y\_0+f(x\_0)h \\cr 1 & x\_0+h & f(x\_0+h) & y\_1 + f(x\_0+h)h \\cr 2 & \\;\\;\\;x\_0+2h\\;\\;\\; & f(x\_0+2h) & y\_2 + f(x\_0+2h)h \\cr \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\cr n & x\_0 + nh & f(x\_0+nh) & y\_n + f(x\_0+nh)h}${"altText":"matrix \[0, x\_0, f(x\_0), y\_0+f(x\_0)h; 1, x\_0+h, f(x\_0+h), y\_1 + f(x\_0+h)h; 2, x\_0+2h, f(x\_0+2h), y\_2 + f(x\_0+2h)h; ..., ..., ..., ...; n, x\_0 + nh, f(x\_0+nh), y\_n + f(x\_0+nh)h\]"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F52E828B] ### Visualizing Euler's Method
syms f(x)
% Define a function such that dy/dx = f(x);
f(x) = x^3-2*x^2+1; %[control:editfield:46e8]{"position":[8,19]}
% Choose the left (x=a) and right (x=b) endpoints of the interval
a =  2;  %[control:editfield:23d8]{"position":[5,7]}
b =  30;   %[control:editfield:6fae]{"position":[5,8]}
% Define the value y(a) = ya
ya =  1; %[control:editfield:9e1d]{"position":[6,8]}
% How many intervals do you want?
intervals = 7; %[control:slider:8719]{"position":[13,14]}
% How much time do you want to pause between steps?
tPause = 0.5;
ShowEulersMethod(@(x)f(x),a,b,intervals,ya,tPause)
%[text] 
%%
