%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7C12DDEB] ## Using Taylor Series to estimate error
%[text] If we begin with a function $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(x)${"altText":"(dy)\/(dx) = f(x)"} and integrate it, we have $\\displaystyle y = \\int f(x)\\; dx${"altText":"y = integral of f(x) dx"}. Consider the Taylor series expansion of the function 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y(x) = y(x\_0)+y'(x\_0)(x-x\_0)+\\frac{y''(x\_0)}{2}(x-x\_0)^2 + \\frac{y'''(x\_0)}{6}(x-x\_0)^3 + \\ldots${"altText":"y(x) = y(x\_0)+y'(x\_0)(x-x\_0)+(y''(x\_0))\/(2)(x-x\_0)^2 + (y'''(x\_0))\/(6)(x-x\_0)^3 + ..."}.
%[text] In this situation, $y(x\_0)=y\_0${"altText":"y(x\_0)=y\_0"} is a known value, and $y'(x\_0) = f(x\_0)${"altText":"y'(x\_0) = f(x\_0)"}, while $(x-x\_0) = h${"altText":"(x-x\_0) = h"}. Therefore, for the Euler method, the theoretical error at each step is approximately:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{Error for one step} = y(x\_0+h)-y\_{\\text{approx}}(x\_0+h) = \\left(y\_0 + f(x\_0)h + \\frac{f'(x\_0)}{2}h^2 + \\mathcal{O}(h^3)\\right) - \\left(y\_0+ f(x\_0)h\\right) = \\frac{f''(x\_0)}{2}h^2${"altText":"Error for one step = y(x\_0+h)-y\_{approx}(x\_0+h) = (y\_0 + f(x\_0)h + (f'(x\_0))\/(2)h^2 + O(h^3)) - (y\_0+ f(x\_0)h) = (f''(x\_0))\/(2)h^2"}.
%[text] To reach the desired $x${"altText":"x"} value from $x\_0${"altText":"x\_0"} we need to take $\\frac{x-x\_0}{h}${"altText":"(x-x\_0)\/(h)"} steps:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{Error at }\\; x = y(x)-y\_{\\text{approx}}(x) \\approx \\left(\\frac{x-x\_0}{h}\\right)\\left( \\frac{f''(x\_0)}{2}h^2+ \\mathcal{O}(h^3)\\right)  =\\left(x-x\_0\\right)\\left(\\frac{f'(x\_0)}{2}\\right)h + \\mathcal{O}(h^2) = \\mathcal{O}(h).${"altText":"Error at x = y(x)-y\_{approx}(x) approximately ((x-x\_0)\/(h))( (f''(x\_0))\/(2)h^2+ O(h^3)) =(x-x\_0)((f'(x\_0))\/(2))h + O(h^2) = O(h)."}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_750D23A5] ## Experiment with a known value to confirm the practical error
%[text] If we choose a simple integrable function close to the function we are numerically integrating, we can calculate some estimates of the error of our method.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4AF6BE92] ### Testing Euler's Method
%[text] Build a table to check the order of the error of an Euler's method solution to an initial value problem $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(x)${"altText":"(dy)\/(dx) = f(x)"} with $y(x\_0)=y\_0${"altText":"y(x\_0)=y\_0"} for different values of $h${"altText":"h"} when estimating the value `y(xEnd)`. 
