%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B6BCB41A] ### Simpson's rule
%[text] When approximating a continuous function from discrete data using a piecewise linear interpolation is not sufficient, you may want to try approximating your function using piecewise polynomials of higher orders. This was the approach taken by Thomas Simpson and Johannes Kepler. \[1,2\]
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Frontispiece of "Essays on Several Curious and Useful Subjects in speculative and mixed Mathematics by Thomas Simpson, Courtesy of Wikimedia Commons and Biblioteca Europea di Informazione e Cultura](text:image:2e07) ![Portrait of Johannes Kepler, Courtesy of the Smithsonian Libraries and Archives](text:image:18f0)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B93457E1] ### ![Try ](text:image:1faf) Visualizing Simpson's rule
syms f(x)
% Define a function such that dy/dx = f(x);
f(x) = (x^3-2*x^2+1)*sin(0.5*x); %[control:editfield:7481]{"position":[8,32]}
% Choose the left (x=a) and right (x=b) endpoints of the interval
a =  2;  %[control:editfield:3b88]{"position":[5,7]}
b =  30;   %[control:editfield:8235]{"position":[5,8]}
% Define the value y(a) = ya
ya =  1; %[control:editfield:6ded]{"position":[6,8]}
% How many intervals do you want?
intervals = 4; %[control:slider:2dca]{"position":[13,14]}
ShowSimpsonsMethod(@(x)f(x),a,b,intervals)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A37F5343] ![](text:image:7204)**Exercise 3: Implement Simpson's rule**
%[text] Approximating our function $f(x)${"altText":"f(x)"} by a quadratic polynomial that passes through the points $x=a${"altText":"x=a"}, $x=b${"altText":"x=b"}, and $x= \\frac{a+b}{2}${"altText":"x= (a+b)\/(2)"}, we end up with the formula
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_8C67784C] $\\displaystyle \\int\_a^b f(x)\\; dx = \\frac{h}{3}\\left(f(a)+4f\\left(\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right)+f(b)\\right)${"altText":"int\_a^b f(x) dx = (h)\/(3)(f(a)+4f((a+b)\/(2))+f(b))"},  where $\\displaystyle h = \\frac{b-a}{2}${"altText":"h = (b-a)\/(2)"}.    $(\\mathcal{S})${"altText":"(S)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] Using [formula $(\\mathcal{S})${"altText":"(S)"}](internal:M_8C67784C), write a function called `simpsonsRule` that will use $n${"altText":"n"} piecewise quadratic functions to approximate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\_a^b f(x)\\; dx${"altText":"int\_a^b f(x) dx"}. 
open("simpsonsRule.m")
%[text] ![](text:image:5a68) **Pro-tip**. To ensure that both `a` and `b` are endpoints of your discretized interval, you should use
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] linspace(a,b,n+1)
%[text] ```
%[text] rather than
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] a:(b-a)/n:b
%[text] ```
%[text] since floating-point representation of `(b-a)/n` can result in `a:(b-a)/n:b` not including the value `b` in some cases.
%%
% Use this code to check the computations of simpsonsRule

% Set up the function, endpoints, and number of subintervals
f = @(x) 3*x.^3-2;
a = 0;
b = 2;
n = 1;

% Run the simpsonsRule approximation code
simpsonsRule(f,a,b,n)
% The exact value of this integral is:
syms x
double(int(f(x),a,b))
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_288CFC44] **Checking Exercise 3: implementation of Simpson's rule**
errors = TestSimpsonsRule(@simpsonsRule);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9594BFBF] ### Empirically Testing the error in Simpson's rule
%[text] Build a table to check the order of the error of an integral over finite bounds $\[a,b\]${"altText":"\[a,b\]"} with different numbers of subintervals, $n${"altText":"n"}. Note that the length of each subinterval is $h = \\frac{b-a}{n}${"altText":"h = (b-a)\/(n)"}.
% Set up the parameters of the problem
f = @(x) exp(x).*cos(x);
a = 0;
b = 1;

% Use the symbolic engine to calculate the integral
syms x
Fexact = int(f(x),a,b);

% Set up a vector of the number of intervals to use
n = 2.^(1:8);
% Define a decreasing column vector of values for h
h = double((b-a)./n)';

FEst = nan(size(h));
for k = 1:length(n)
    FEst(k) = simpsonsRule(f,a,b,n(k));
end
FErr = double(Fexact-FEst);

ratio = [nan; FErr(2:end)./FErr(1:end-1)];
estOrd = -log2(ratio);
vals = [h FErr ratio estOrd];
format long
T = array2table(vals(2:end,:),"VariableNames",...
    ["h", "Error", "Ratio", "Estimated Order"])
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F2D942EC] #### ![](text:image:44cc) **Reflect: Expected Error for Simpson's Rule**
%[text] 1. Given the estimated order you calculated with $f(x) = e^x\\cos(x)${"altText":"f(x) = e^xcos(x)"} and the connection of the order of error to the Taylor series, which calculations can be computed exactly using Simpson's rule with $n=1${"altText":"n=1"}. Why or why not? \
%[text]{"align":"center"} a) $\\displaystyle \\int\_0^4 \\sin(x)\\; dx${"altText":"int\_0^4 sin(x) dx"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} b) $\\displaystyle \\int\_0^1 x^2-\\frac{1}{6}x+3\\; dx${"altText":"int\_0^1 x^2-(1)\/(6)x+3 dx"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} c) $\\displaystyle \\int\_{-7}^{13} 0.53x^3+1.7x^2-8x+2\\; dx${"altText":"int\_{-7}^{13} 0.53x^3+1.7x^2-8x+2 dx"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} d) $\\displaystyle \\int\_{-1}^{1} x\\; dx${"altText":"int\_{-1}^{1} x dx"} 
%[text]           2\. When the error is 0, what is the optimal step size to choose?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_123A640E] ## What happens if the step size is constrained?
%[text] In all of the examples of numerical methods so far, except for `ode23s`, we have assumed that the step size is identical throughout the solution. This is not a reasonable assumption. How can you modify the solvers you have created to work with arbitrary step sizes? 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_54F347D0] ### Modifying Euler's method
%[text] Euler's method is quite simple to update. While the basic formula was
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\int\_a^b f(x)\\; dx \\approx \\sum\_{k=1}^n f(x\_k)\\left(\\frac{b-a}{n}\\right)${"altText":"int\_a^b f(x) dx approximately sum\_{k=1}^n f(x\_k)((b-a)\/(n))"},      with $\\displaystyle x\_k = a+(k-1)\\left(\\frac{b-a}{n}\\right)${"altText":"x\_k = a+(k-1)((b-a)\/(n))"},
%[text] if the $k^{\\text{th}}${"altText":"k^{th}"} interval is $\\displaystyle \[x\_k,x\_{k+1}\]${"altText":"\[x\_k,x\_{k+1}\]"}, with $x\_1 = a${"altText":"x\_1 = a"} and $x\_{n+1} = b${"altText":"x\_{n+1} = b"}, then 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\int\_a^b f(x)\\; dx = \\sum\_{k=1}^n f(x\_k)\\left(x\_{k+1}-x\_k\\right).${"altText":"int\_a^b f(x) dx = sum\_{k=1}^n f(x\_k)(x\_{k+1}-x\_k)."}
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8A1EA61E] ### Modifying Simpson's rule
%[text] Simpson's rule takes a bit more effort to update because we need to think carefully about what it means to fit a quadratic through the points that we know. There will be different cases for Simpson's rule depending on whether we have an even or odd number of sub-intervals. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E2AFF1BD] #### Even number of sub-intervals
%[text] If we have an even number of subintervals, $S${"altText":"S"}, and thus the $k^{\\text{th}}${"altText":"k^{th}"} subinterval is $\[x\_k,x\_{k+1}\]${"altText":"\[x\_k,x\_{k+1}\]"}, we can compute a Simpson's rule calculation with $\\displaystyle n = \\frac{S}{2}${"altText":"n = (S)\/(2)"} and $h\_{m} = x\_{m+1}-x\_m${"altText":"h\_{m} = x\_{m+1}-x\_m"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\int\_a^b f(x)\\; dx \\approx \\sum\_{k=1}^n \\frac{x\_{2k+1}-x\_{2k-1}}{6}\\left\[\\left(2-\\frac{h\_{2k}}{h\_{2k-1}}\\right)f(x\_{2k-1}) + \\left(\\frac{(x\_{2k+1}-x\_{2k-1})^2}{h\_{2k}h\_{2k-1}}\\right)f(x\_{2k}) + \\left(2-\\frac{h\_{2k-1}}{h\_{2k}}\\right)f(x\_{2k+1})\\right\]${"altText":"int\_a^b f(x) dx approximately sum\_{k=1}^n (x\_{2k+1}-x\_{2k-1})\/(6)\[(2-(h\_{2k})\/(h\_{2k-1}))f(x\_{2k-1}) + (((x\_{2k+1}-x\_{2k-1})^2)\/(h\_{2k}h\_{2k-1}))f(x\_{2k}) + (2-(h\_{2k-1})\/(h\_{2k}))f(x\_{2k+1})\]"}.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8BABB653] #### Odd number of sub-intervals
%[text] If we have an odd number of subintervals, we cannot pair them all up to create appropriate quadratic curves through each triplet. One way to address this case is to drop the final subinterval to reduce ourselves to the even subintervals case and then use the last two subintervals to create an approximate quadratic to use in the numerical integration of the final subinterval.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7FBDE97F] ### Cannot update Gaussian quadrature
%[text] Gaussian quadrature relies on knowing values at particular points. Gaussian quadrature cannot be used if those integrand values are not known.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F6743F93] ### Application of interpolation
%[text] One method that can be used to regularize irregular data for use in standard numerical methods is to interpolate the necessary values at the desired evenly-spaced points. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8672F952] ## References
%[text] \[1\] Thomas Simpson, [Simpson, Thomas – Essays on several curious and useful subjects, in speculative and mix'd mathematicks, 1740 – BEIC 768468](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simpson,_Thomas_%E2%80%93_Essays_on_several_curious_and_useful_subjects,_in_speculative_and_mix'd_mathematicks,_1740_%E2%80%93_BEIC_768468.jpg), marked as public domain, more details on [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-old) Accessed Apr 14, 2022.
%[text] \[2\] Portrait of Johannes Kepler. Photogravure by Photographische Gesellschaft, Berlin (Photographic company), published by Photographische Gesellschaft, Berlin (Photographic company). [https://library.si.edu/image-gallery/72833](https://library.si.edu/image-gallery/72833) Accessed Apr 14. 2022.
%%
